1
00:00:01,639 --> 00:00:02,720
Chapter 36.

2
00:00:03,880 --> 00:00:05,179
The daylight came.

3
00:00:06,259 --> 00:00:07,259
I rose at dawn.

4
00:00:08,259 --> 00:00:11,619
I busied myself for an hour or two with arranging my things in my chamber,

5
00:00:11,860 --> 00:00:14,165
drawers, and wardrobe, in the order wherein I

6
00:00:14,167 --> 00:00:17,080
should wish to leave them during a brief absence.

7
00:00:18,539 --> 00:00:21,440
Meantime, I heard Sinjin quit his room.

8
00:00:22,160 --> 00:00:23,160
He stopped at my door.

9
00:00:24,059 --> 00:00:25,199
I feared he would knock.

10
00:00:26,039 --> 00:00:29,300
No, but a slip of paper was passed under the door.

11
00:00:30,059 --> 00:00:31,059
I took it up.

12
00:00:31,479 --> 00:00:32,500
It bore these words.

13
00:00:33,940 --> 00:00:36,199
You left me too suddenly last night.

14
00:00:37,380 --> 00:00:40,376
Had you stayed but a little longer, you would have laid

15
00:00:40,376 --> 00:00:43,600
your hand on the Christians cross and the angels crown.

16
00:00:45,059 --> 00:00:48,360
I shall expect your clear decision when I return this day fortnight.

17
00:00:49,580 --> 00:00:53,919
Meantime, watch and pray that you enter not into temptation.

18
00:00:55,240 --> 00:00:59,559
The spirit I trust is willing, but the flesh I see is weak.

19
00:01:00,479 --> 00:01:03,600
I shall pray for you hourly, yours, Sinjin.

20
00:01:05,360 --> 00:01:10,240
My spirit, I answered mentally, is willing to do what is right.

21
00:01:11,119 --> 00:01:14,682
And my flesh, I hope, is strong enough to accomplish the

22
00:01:14,683 --> 00:01:18,059
will of heaven when once that will is distinctly known to me.

23
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:23,660
At any rate, it shall be strong enough to search, inquire, to grow an outlet from

24
00:01:23,660 --> 00:01:27,880
this cloud of doubt and find the open day of certainty.

25
00:01:30,119 --> 00:01:31,119
And Jin?

26
00:01:31,919 --> 00:01:35,627
It was the first of June, yet the morning was

27
00:01:35,629 --> 00:01:39,740
overcast and chilly, rain beat fast on my casement.

28
00:01:40,119 --> 00:01:44,279
I heard the front door open, and Sinjin pass out.

29
00:01:45,440 --> 00:01:48,779
Looking through the window, I saw him traverse the garden.

30
00:01:49,720 --> 00:01:52,478
He took the way over the misty mowers in the

31
00:01:52,479 --> 00:01:55,880
direction of Whitcross, there he would meet the coach.

32
00:01:56,860 --> 00:02:00,800
In a few more hours, I shall succeed you in that track cousin, thought I.

33
00:02:01,899 --> 00:02:04,080
I too have a coach to meet at Whitcross.

34
00:02:04,960 --> 00:02:10,520
I too have some to see and ask after in England, before I depart forever.

35
00:02:11,639 --> 00:02:14,800
It wanted yet two hours of breakfast time.

36
00:02:15,699 --> 00:02:20,660
I filled the interval in walking softly about my room, and pondering the

37
00:02:20,660 --> 00:02:23,479
visitation which had given my plans their present vent.

38
00:02:24,960 --> 00:02:30,320
I recalled that inward sensation I had experienced, for I could recall it,

39
00:02:30,720 --> 00:02:32,919
with all its unspeakable strangeness.

40
00:02:33,639 --> 00:02:35,600
I recalled the voice I had heard.

41
00:02:36,240 --> 00:02:40,300
Again, I questioned whence it came, as vainly as before.

42
00:02:41,259 --> 00:02:44,259
It seemed in me, not in the external world.

43
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:49,440
I asked, was it a mere nervous impression, a delusion?

44
00:02:50,399 --> 00:02:52,199
I could not conceive or believe.

45
00:02:53,220 --> 00:02:55,080
It was more like an inspiration.

46
00:02:56,279 --> 00:02:59,754
The wondrous shock of feeling had come like the earthquake

47
00:02:59,755 --> 00:03:02,880
which shook the foundations of Paul and Silas' prison.

48
00:03:03,779 --> 00:03:07,419
It had opened the doors of the soul cell and loosed its bands.

49
00:03:08,039 --> 00:03:12,240
It had wakened it out of its sleep, whence it sprang trembling, listening,

50
00:03:12,460 --> 00:03:18,779
aghast, then vibrated thrice a cry on my startled ear, and in my quaking heart and

51
00:03:18,781 --> 00:03:24,800
through my spirit, which neither feared nor shook, but exalted as if in joy over

52
00:03:24,801 --> 00:03:27,979
the success of one effort it had been privileged to make.

53
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:36,581
Independent of the comberest body, air many days, I said, as I terminated my musings.

54
00:03:36,800 --> 00:03:41,339
I will know something of him whose voice seemed last night to summon me.

55
00:03:42,380 --> 00:03:46,740
Letters have proved of no avail, personal inquiry shall replace them.

56
00:03:47,539 --> 00:03:51,013
At breakfast I announced to Diana and Mary that I was

57
00:03:51,013 --> 00:03:54,820
going a journey and should be absent at least four days.

58
00:03:56,619 --> 00:04:01,586
Alone Jane, they asked, yes, it was to see or hear news

59
00:04:01,588 --> 00:04:05,399
of a friend about whom I had for some time been uneasy.

60
00:04:06,619 --> 00:04:10,493
They might have said, as I have no doubt they thought, that

61
00:04:10,494 --> 00:04:13,539
they had believed me to be without any friends save them.

62
00:04:14,460 --> 00:04:19,919
For indeed, I had often said so, but with their true natural delicacy,

63
00:04:20,539 --> 00:04:23,570
they abstained from comment, except that Diana

64
00:04:23,572 --> 00:04:26,680
asked me if I was sure I was well enough to travel.

65
00:04:27,460 --> 00:04:29,979
I looked very pale, she observed.

66
00:04:30,660 --> 00:04:34,577
I replied that nothing ailed me save anxiety

67
00:04:34,578 --> 00:04:38,301
of mind, which I hoped soon to alleviate.

68
00:04:38,699 --> 00:04:42,164
It was easy to make my further arrangements, for

69
00:04:42,164 --> 00:04:46,279
I was troubled with no inquiries, no surmises.

70
00:04:47,500 --> 00:04:52,000
Having once explained to them that I could not now be explicit about my plans,

71
00:04:52,759 --> 00:04:57,600
they kindly and wisely acquiesced in the silence with which I pursued them.

72
00:04:58,320 --> 00:05:01,381
According to me, the privilege of free action I

73
00:05:01,383 --> 00:05:04,979
should under similar circumstances have accorded them.

74
00:05:06,199 --> 00:05:12,339
I left Morehouse at 3 o'clock P.M., and soon after four I stood at the foot of

75
00:05:12,341 --> 00:05:16,282
the signpost of Whitcross, waiting the arrival of the

76
00:05:16,282 --> 00:05:20,059
coach, which was to take me to distant Thornfield.

77
00:05:21,259 --> 00:05:25,302
Amidst the silence of those solitary roads and desert

78
00:05:25,304 --> 00:05:28,660
hills, I heard it approach from a great distance.

79
00:05:29,600 --> 00:05:32,160
It was the same vehicle went a year ago.

80
00:05:32,779 --> 00:05:36,278
I had a lighted one summer evening on this very spot, how

81
00:05:36,279 --> 00:05:41,440
desolate and hopeless and objectless it stopped as I beckoned.

82
00:05:42,399 --> 00:05:43,399
I entered.

83
00:05:44,059 --> 00:05:48,500
Not now obliged to part with my whole fortune as the price of its accommodation.

84
00:05:50,039 --> 00:05:55,801
Once more on the road to Thornfield, I felt like the messenger pigeon flying home.

85
00:05:56,919 --> 00:06:00,199
It was a journey of six and thirty hours.

86
00:06:01,240 --> 00:06:06,500
I had set out from Whitcross on a Tuesday afternoon, and early on the succeeding

87
00:06:06,501 --> 00:06:11,139
Thursday morning, the coach stopped toward the horses at a wayside in.

88
00:06:11,979 --> 00:06:16,580
Situated in the midst of scenery whose green hedges and large fields and low

89
00:06:16,581 --> 00:06:22,479
pastoral hills, how mild of feature and verdant of hue compared with the stern

90
00:06:22,480 --> 00:06:25,754
North Midland Moors of Morton, met my eye

91
00:06:25,755 --> 00:06:28,919
like the liniments of a once -familiar face.

92
00:06:29,679 --> 00:06:32,360
Yes, I knew the character of this landscape.

93
00:06:33,059 --> 00:06:35,020
I was sure we were near my born.

94
00:06:37,080 --> 00:06:39,520
How far is Thornfield Hall from here?

95
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:41,100
I asked of the Osler.

96
00:06:42,339 --> 00:06:44,559
Just two miles man across the fields.

97
00:06:45,880 --> 00:06:48,720
My journey is closed, I thought to myself.

98
00:06:49,479 --> 00:06:54,660
I got out of the coach, gave a box I had into the Osler's charge to be kept till I

99
00:06:54,660 --> 00:06:59,479
called for it, paid my fare, satisfied the coachman and was going.

100
00:07:00,360 --> 00:07:05,520
The brightening day gleamed on the sign of the inn, and I read in guilt letters,

101
00:07:06,300 --> 00:07:07,860
the Rochester arms.

102
00:07:08,759 --> 00:07:10,520
My heart leapt up.

103
00:07:11,199 --> 00:07:13,380
I was already on my master's very lands.

104
00:07:13,940 --> 00:07:14,940
It fell again.

105
00:07:15,600 --> 00:07:17,059
The thought struck it.

106
00:07:17,061 --> 00:07:22,039
Your master himself may be beyond the British Channel, for ought you know.

107
00:07:22,460 --> 00:07:25,555
And then, if he is at Thornfield Hall, towards

108
00:07:25,557 --> 00:07:29,001
which you hasten, who besides him is there.

109
00:07:30,160 --> 00:07:31,720
His lunatic wife.

110
00:07:32,419 --> 00:07:34,320
And you have nothing to do with him.

111
00:07:34,880 --> 00:07:37,179
You dare not speak to him or seek his presence.

112
00:07:38,119 --> 00:07:39,279
You have lost your labour.

113
00:07:40,660 --> 00:07:42,000
You would better go no farther.

114
00:07:42,800 --> 00:07:43,800
Urged the monitor.

115
00:07:44,600 --> 00:07:46,380
Ask information of the people at the inn.

116
00:07:47,059 --> 00:07:48,479
They can give you all you seek.

117
00:07:49,240 --> 00:07:51,020
They can solve your doubts at once.

118
00:07:52,059 --> 00:07:56,139
Go up to that man, and inquire if Mr. Rochester be at home.

119
00:07:57,639 --> 00:07:59,479
The suggestion was sensible.

120
00:08:00,300 --> 00:08:03,320
And yet, I could not force myself to act on it.

121
00:08:03,880 --> 00:08:07,020
I so dreaded a reply that would crush me with despair.

122
00:08:08,059 --> 00:08:10,600
To prolong doubt was to prolong hope.

123
00:08:11,619 --> 00:08:15,660
I might yet once more see the hall under the ray of her star.

124
00:08:17,460 --> 00:08:19,360
There was the style before me.

125
00:08:20,179 --> 00:08:23,619
The very fields through which I had hurried blind deaf, distracted,

126
00:08:24,059 --> 00:08:26,960
with a revengeful fury tracking and scourging me.

127
00:08:27,639 --> 00:08:29,940
On the morning I fled from Thornfield.

128
00:08:30,940 --> 00:08:33,639
Air I well knew what course I had resolved to take.

129
00:08:34,059 --> 00:08:35,160
I was in the midst of them.

130
00:08:36,419 --> 00:08:38,380
How fast I walked.

131
00:08:39,039 --> 00:08:40,380
How I ran sometimes.

132
00:08:41,279 --> 00:08:45,139
How I looked forward to catch the first view of the well-known woods.

133
00:08:46,160 --> 00:08:49,797
With what feelings I welcomed single trees I knew,

134
00:08:49,797 --> 00:08:53,059
and familiar glimpses of meadow and hill between them.

135
00:08:53,899 --> 00:08:55,399
At last the woods rose.

136
00:08:56,360 --> 00:08:57,940
The ruckery clustered dark.

137
00:08:58,720 --> 00:09:01,380
A loud cawing broke the morning stillness.

138
00:09:02,300 --> 00:09:05,800
Strange delight inspired me, on I hastened.

139
00:09:06,679 --> 00:09:11,259
Another field crossed, a lane threaded, and there were the courtyard walls,

140
00:09:11,820 --> 00:09:15,479
the back offices, the house itself, the ruckery still hid.

141
00:09:17,220 --> 00:09:21,039
My first view of it shall be in front, I determined.

142
00:09:21,960 --> 00:09:25,358
Where its bold battlements will strike the eye nobly at

143
00:09:25,360 --> 00:09:29,199
once, and where I can single out my master's very window.

144
00:09:30,460 --> 00:09:33,779
Perhaps he will be standing at it, he rises early.

145
00:09:34,539 --> 00:09:37,699
Perhaps he is now walking in the orchard or on the pavement in front.

146
00:09:38,660 --> 00:09:39,979
Could I but see him?

147
00:09:40,699 --> 00:09:41,699
But a moment.

148
00:09:42,220 --> 00:09:46,479
Surely in that case I should not be so mad as to run to him.

149
00:09:46,960 --> 00:09:48,800
I cannot tell, I am not certain.

150
00:09:49,460 --> 00:09:50,460
And if I did?

151
00:09:50,979 --> 00:09:51,979
What then?

152
00:09:52,500 --> 00:09:53,779
God bless him.

153
00:09:54,160 --> 00:09:54,679
What then?

154
00:09:55,160 --> 00:09:59,559
Who would be hurt by my once more tasting the life his glance can give me?

155
00:10:00,300 --> 00:10:01,300
I rave.

156
00:10:01,720 --> 00:10:05,027
Perhaps at this moment he is watching the sunrise over

157
00:10:05,028 --> 00:10:07,960
the Pyrenees, or on the tideless sea of the south.

158
00:10:09,039 --> 00:10:13,139
I had coasted along the lower wall of the orchard, turned its angle.

159
00:10:13,899 --> 00:10:17,330
There was a gate just there, opening into the meadow,

160
00:10:17,331 --> 00:10:21,059
between two stone pillars crowned by stone balls.

161
00:10:22,100 --> 00:10:27,800
From behind one pillar, I could peep round quietly at the full front of the mansion.

162
00:10:28,779 --> 00:10:34,279
I advanced my head with precaution, desirous to ascertain if any bedroom

163
00:10:34,281 --> 00:10:39,300
window blinds were yet drawn up, battlements, windows, long front,

164
00:10:40,100 --> 00:10:43,740
all from this sheltered station were at my command.

165
00:10:44,860 --> 00:10:48,740
The crows sailing overhead perhaps watched me while I took this survey.

166
00:10:49,399 --> 00:10:50,860
I wonder what they thought.

167
00:10:51,580 --> 00:10:56,380
They must have considered I was very careful and timid at first, and that

168
00:10:56,380 --> 00:11:03,080
gradually I grew very bold and reckless, a peep, and then a long stare,

169
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:09,059
and then a departure from my niche and a straying out into the meadow, and a sudden

170
00:11:09,061 --> 00:11:15,039
stop, full in front of the great mansion, and a protracted hardy gaze towards it.

171
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:19,419
What affectation of dividends was this at first?

172
00:11:19,899 --> 00:11:21,119
They might have demanded.

173
00:11:21,580 --> 00:11:24,039
What stupid regardlessness now?

174
00:11:25,919 --> 00:11:27,860
Here an illustration, reader.

175
00:11:29,179 --> 00:11:32,460
A lover finds his mistress asleep on a mossy bank.

176
00:11:33,279 --> 00:11:36,419
He wishes to catch a glimpse of her fair face without waking her.

177
00:11:37,479 --> 00:11:41,559
He steals softly over the grass, careful to make no sound.

178
00:11:42,240 --> 00:11:44,779
He pauses, fancing she has stirred.

179
00:11:45,539 --> 00:11:48,600
He withdraws, not for worlds would he be seen.

180
00:11:49,520 --> 00:11:50,520
All is still.

181
00:11:51,179 --> 00:11:52,360
He again advances.

182
00:11:53,179 --> 00:11:54,179
He bends above her.

183
00:11:54,479 --> 00:11:56,320
A light veil rests on her features.

184
00:11:56,679 --> 00:11:58,000
He lifts it bends lower.

185
00:11:58,940 --> 00:12:05,520
Now his eyes anticipate the vision of beauty, warm and blooming and lovely in rest.

186
00:12:05,639 --> 00:12:09,100
How hurried was their first glance, but how they fix.

187
00:12:09,940 --> 00:12:10,940
How he starts.

188
00:12:11,440 --> 00:12:14,622
How he suddenly and vehemently clasps in both arms the

189
00:12:14,623 --> 00:12:18,320
form he dared not a moment since, touch with his finger.

190
00:12:18,880 --> 00:12:23,880
How he calls a loud a name, and drops his burden and gazes on it wildly.

191
00:12:24,440 --> 00:12:27,562
He thus grasps and cries and gazes, because he no

192
00:12:27,562 --> 00:12:30,580
longer fears to waken by any sound he can utter.

193
00:12:31,360 --> 00:12:33,559
By any movement he can make.

194
00:12:34,660 --> 00:12:36,639
He thought his love slept sweetly.

195
00:12:37,479 --> 00:12:39,259
He finds she is stone dead.

196
00:12:40,300 --> 00:12:43,500
I looked with timorous joy towards a stately house.

197
00:12:44,100 --> 00:12:45,779
I saw a blackened ruin.

198
00:12:47,639 --> 00:12:50,240
No need to cower behind a gatepost, indeed.

199
00:12:51,020 --> 00:12:55,179
To peep up at chamber lattices, fearing life was a stir behind them.

200
00:12:55,960 --> 00:12:58,000
No need to listen for doors opening.

201
00:12:58,740 --> 00:13:01,380
To fancy steps on the pavement or the gravel walk.

202
00:13:02,500 --> 00:13:05,059
The lawn, the grounds were trodden and waste.

203
00:13:05,779 --> 00:13:07,160
The portal yawned void.

204
00:13:08,179 --> 00:13:10,979
The front was, as I had once seen it in a dream.

205
00:13:11,820 --> 00:13:15,653
But a shell-like wall, very high and very fragile

206
00:13:15,653 --> 00:13:19,520
looking, perforated with panellous windows.

207
00:13:19,779 --> 00:13:23,720
No roof, no battlements, no chimneys, all had crashed in.

208
00:13:24,660 --> 00:13:26,940
And there was the silence of death about it.

209
00:13:27,660 --> 00:13:29,880
The solitude of a lonesome wild.

210
00:13:30,919 --> 00:13:36,500
No wonder that letters addressed to people here had never received an answer.

211
00:13:37,380 --> 00:13:40,839
As well dispatched epistles to a vault in a church aisle.

212
00:13:41,919 --> 00:13:45,210
The grim blackness of the stones told by what

213
00:13:45,211 --> 00:13:48,180
fate the hall had fallen by conflagration.

214
00:13:48,880 --> 00:13:50,080
But how kindled.

215
00:13:50,820 --> 00:13:52,979
What story belonged to this disaster?

216
00:13:53,899 --> 00:13:58,539
What loss, besides mortar and marble and woodwork had followed upon it?

217
00:13:59,399 --> 00:14:01,899
Had life been wrecked as well as property?

218
00:14:02,779 --> 00:14:04,440
If so, who's?

219
00:14:05,279 --> 00:14:06,279
Dreadful question.

220
00:14:07,160 --> 00:14:11,580
There was no one here to answer it, not even dumb sign, mute token.

221
00:14:13,020 --> 00:14:17,160
In wandering round the shattered walls and through the devastated interior,

222
00:14:18,100 --> 00:14:21,839
I gathered evidence that the calamity was not of late occurrence.

223
00:14:23,320 --> 00:14:27,979
Winter snows, I thought, had drifted through that void arch.

224
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:32,059
Winter rains beaten in at those hollow casements.

225
00:14:32,580 --> 00:14:38,399
For amidst the drenched piles of rubbish, spring had cherished vegetation,

226
00:14:39,199 --> 00:14:43,220
grass and weed grew here and there between the stones and fallen rafters.

227
00:14:44,039 --> 00:14:48,059
And oh, where meantime was the hapless owner of this wreck?

228
00:14:48,740 --> 00:14:49,740
In what land?

229
00:14:50,080 --> 00:14:51,199
Under what auspices?

230
00:14:52,039 --> 00:14:56,520
My eye involuntarily wandered to the church tower near the gates.

231
00:14:57,339 --> 00:15:00,453
And I asked, is he with Damon de Rochester,

232
00:15:00,455 --> 00:15:03,699
sharing the shelter of his narrow marble house?

233
00:15:05,259 --> 00:15:07,839
Some answer must be had to these questions.

234
00:15:08,639 --> 00:15:10,500
I could find it nowhere but at the in.

235
00:15:11,419 --> 00:15:13,860
And the other, air-long, I returned.

236
00:15:15,300 --> 00:15:18,559
The host himself brought my breakfast into the parlour.

237
00:15:19,179 --> 00:15:21,899
I requested him to shut the door and sit down.

238
00:15:22,740 --> 00:15:24,820
I had some questions to ask him.

239
00:15:25,419 --> 00:15:28,779
But when he complied, I scarcely knew how to begin.

240
00:15:29,759 --> 00:15:32,759
Such horror had I of the possible answers.

241
00:15:33,800 --> 00:15:37,111
And yet the spectacle of desolation I had just

242
00:15:37,111 --> 00:15:40,919
left prepared me in a measure for a tale of misery.

243
00:15:42,100 --> 00:15:45,020
The host was a respectable-looking, middle-aged man.

244
00:15:46,080 --> 00:15:47,679
You know Thornfield Hall, of course?

245
00:15:48,199 --> 00:15:49,919
I managed to say it last.

246
00:15:50,699 --> 00:15:52,259
Yes, ma'am, I lived there once.

247
00:15:53,139 --> 00:15:54,139
Did you?

248
00:15:54,740 --> 00:15:55,979
Not in my time, I thought.

249
00:15:56,440 --> 00:15:57,679
You are a stranger to me.

250
00:15:58,800 --> 00:16:01,080
I was the late Mr. Rochester's butler.

251
00:16:01,600 --> 00:16:02,600
He added.

252
00:16:03,360 --> 00:16:05,563
The late... I seem to have received with full

253
00:16:05,565 --> 00:16:08,740
force the blow I had been trying to evade.

254
00:16:09,139 --> 00:16:10,960
The late, I gasped.

255
00:16:11,300 --> 00:16:12,300
Is he dead?

256
00:16:12,899 --> 00:16:16,300
I mean the present gentleman, Mr. Edward's father, he explained.

257
00:16:17,080 --> 00:16:18,080
I breathed again.

258
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:20,539
My blood resumed its flow.

259
00:16:21,699 --> 00:16:27,520
Fully assured by these words that Mr. Edward, my Mr. Rochester, God bless him,

260
00:16:27,779 --> 00:16:33,779
wherever he was, was at least alive, was in short, the present gentleman.

261
00:16:35,300 --> 00:16:39,139
Gladdening words, it seemed I could hear all that was to come.

262
00:16:39,880 --> 00:16:43,379
Whatever the disclosures might be, with comparative

263
00:16:43,380 --> 00:16:46,160
tranquility, since he was not in the grave.

264
00:16:46,899 --> 00:16:50,660
I could bear, I thought, to learn that he was at the Antipodes.

265
00:16:52,520 --> 00:16:55,779
Is Mr. Rochester living at Thornfield Hall now?

266
00:16:56,320 --> 00:16:59,880
I asked, knowing, of course, what the answer would be.

267
00:17:00,520 --> 00:17:05,460
But yet, desirous of deferring the direct question as to where he really was.

268
00:17:06,779 --> 00:17:10,360
No, ma'am, oh no, no one is living there.

269
00:17:11,160 --> 00:17:13,426
I suppose you are a stranger in these parts, or

270
00:17:13,426 --> 00:17:15,940
you would have heard what happened last autumn.

271
00:17:17,320 --> 00:17:19,660
Thornfield Hall is quite a ruin.

272
00:17:20,639 --> 00:17:22,740
It was burnt down just about harvest time.

273
00:17:23,660 --> 00:17:28,880
A dreadful calamity, such an immense quantity of valuable property destroyed.

274
00:17:29,520 --> 00:17:31,860
Hardly any of the furniture could be saved.

275
00:17:32,619 --> 00:17:34,740
The fire broke out at dead of night.

276
00:17:35,380 --> 00:17:40,419
And before the engines arrived from Millcott, the building was one mass of flame.

277
00:17:40,919 --> 00:17:42,380
It was a terrible spectacle.

278
00:17:42,839 --> 00:17:43,839
I witnessed it myself.

279
00:17:45,619 --> 00:17:47,800
At dead of night, I muttered?

280
00:17:48,440 --> 00:17:52,139
Yes, that was ever the hour of fatality at Thornfield.

281
00:17:52,979 --> 00:17:55,300
Was it known how it originated?

282
00:17:56,300 --> 00:17:57,300
I demanded.

283
00:17:58,320 --> 00:18:01,100
They guessed, ma'am, they guessed.

284
00:18:01,880 --> 00:18:05,360
Indeed, I should say it was ascertained beyond a doubt.

285
00:18:06,520 --> 00:18:07,940
You are not perhaps aware?

286
00:18:08,779 --> 00:18:12,860
He continued, edging his chair a little nearer the table and speaking low.

287
00:18:13,580 --> 00:18:18,300
That there was a lady, a lunatic kept in the house?

288
00:18:19,160 --> 00:18:20,979
I have heard something of it.

289
00:18:22,139 --> 00:18:24,820
She was kept in very close confinement, ma'am.

290
00:18:25,520 --> 00:18:29,539
People even for some years was not absolutely certain of her existence.

291
00:18:30,479 --> 00:18:31,639
No one saw her.

292
00:18:32,399 --> 00:18:36,080
They only knew by rumor that such a person was at the hall.

293
00:18:36,880 --> 00:18:40,600
And who or what she was, it was difficult to conjecture.

294
00:18:41,539 --> 00:18:44,316
They said Mr. Edward had brought her from abroad,

295
00:18:44,317 --> 00:18:47,520
and some believed she had been his mistress.

296
00:18:48,179 --> 00:18:50,259
But a queer thing happened a year since.

297
00:18:50,919 --> 00:18:52,440
A very queer thing.

298
00:18:53,460 --> 00:18:56,119
I feared now to hear my own story.

299
00:18:56,899 --> 00:18:59,339
I endeavored to recall him to the main fact.

300
00:19:00,279 --> 00:19:06,899
And this lady, this lady, ma'am, he answered, turned out to be Mr.

301
00:19:07,160 --> 00:19:08,160
Rochester's wife.

302
00:19:08,839 --> 00:19:11,639
The discovery was brought about in the strangest way.

303
00:19:12,520 --> 00:19:17,279
There was a young lady, a governess at the hall that Mr. Rochester fell in.

304
00:19:18,100 --> 00:19:20,600
But the fire, I suggested.

305
00:19:21,639 --> 00:19:25,520
I'm coming to that, ma'am, that Mr. Edward fell in love with.

306
00:19:26,360 --> 00:19:30,220
The servants say they never saw anybody so much in love as he was.

307
00:19:30,820 --> 00:19:33,020
He was after her continually.

308
00:19:33,919 --> 00:19:35,279
They used to watch him.

309
00:19:35,779 --> 00:19:37,259
Servants will, you know, ma'am.

310
00:19:38,160 --> 00:19:40,139
And he set store on her past everything.

311
00:19:40,880 --> 00:19:45,259
For all nobody but him thought her so very handsome.

312
00:19:46,100 --> 00:19:50,039
She was a little small thing, they say, almost like a child.

313
00:19:51,139 --> 00:19:56,320
I never saw her myself, but I've heard Lea, the housemaid tell of her.

314
00:19:57,759 --> 00:19:58,979
Lea liked her well enough.

315
00:20:00,059 --> 00:20:04,139
Mr. Rochester was about 40, and this governess not 20.

316
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:09,273
And you see, when gentlemen of his age fall in love with

317
00:20:09,274 --> 00:20:12,300
girls, they are often like as if they were bewitched.

318
00:20:12,940 --> 00:20:15,960
Well, he would marry her.

319
00:20:18,020 --> 00:20:20,779
You shall tell me this part of the story another time, I said.

320
00:20:21,699 --> 00:20:25,919
But now I have a particular reason for wishing to hear all about the fire.

321
00:20:26,899 --> 00:20:32,660
Was it suspected that this lunatic Mrs. Rochester had any hand in it?

322
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:35,440
You've hit it, ma'am.

323
00:20:36,100 --> 00:20:41,000
It's quite certain that it was her, and nobody but her, that said it going.

324
00:20:41,940 --> 00:20:44,880
She had a woman to take care of her called Mrs. Pool.

325
00:20:46,119 --> 00:20:50,339
An able woman in her line, and very trustworthy, but for one fault,

326
00:20:50,919 --> 00:20:55,960
a fault common to a deal of them nurses and matrons, she kept a private bottle of

327
00:20:55,960 --> 00:20:59,419
gin by her, and now and then took a drop over much.

328
00:21:00,500 --> 00:21:01,820
It is excusable.

329
00:21:02,559 --> 00:21:08,139
For she had a hard life of it, but still it was dangerous, for when Mrs.

330
00:21:08,279 --> 00:21:14,919
Pool was fast asleep after the gin and water, the mad lady, who was as cunning as

331
00:21:14,921 --> 00:21:20,000
a witch, would take the keys out of her pocket, let herself out of her chamber,

332
00:21:20,779 --> 00:21:25,339
and go roaming about the house doing any wild mischief that came into her head.

333
00:21:26,639 --> 00:21:29,372
They say she had nearly burnt her husband in

334
00:21:29,373 --> 00:21:33,081
his bed once, but I don't know about that.

335
00:21:33,660 --> 00:21:41,020
However, on this night, she set fire first to the hangings of the room next to her

336
00:21:41,020 --> 00:21:45,611
own, and then she got down to a lower story and made

337
00:21:45,612 --> 00:21:49,000
her way to the chamber that had been the governesses.

338
00:21:49,979 --> 00:21:55,580
She was like as if she knew somehow how matters had gone on, and had a spider to

339
00:21:55,581 --> 00:22:00,800
her, and she kindled the bed there, but there was nobody sleeping in it,

340
00:22:01,339 --> 00:22:02,339
fortunately.

341
00:22:03,059 --> 00:22:08,539
The governess had run away two months before, and for all Mr. Rochester sought

342
00:22:08,540 --> 00:22:12,820
her, as if she had been the most precious thing he had in the world.

343
00:22:13,600 --> 00:22:15,559
He never could hear a word of her.

344
00:22:15,919 --> 00:22:19,860
And he grew savage, quite savage on his disappointment.

345
00:22:20,759 --> 00:22:25,100
He never was a wild man, but he got dangerous after he lost her.

346
00:22:25,919 --> 00:22:27,119
He would be alone too.

347
00:22:28,839 --> 00:22:33,839
He sent Mrs. Fairfax, the housekeeper, away to her friends at a distance.

348
00:22:34,839 --> 00:22:38,615
But he did it handsomely, for he settled an

349
00:22:38,616 --> 00:22:42,160
annuity on her for life, and she deserved it.

350
00:22:42,160 --> 00:22:44,039
She was a very good woman.

351
00:22:44,779 --> 00:22:48,860
Miss Adele, a ward he had, was put to school.

352
00:22:49,720 --> 00:22:51,942
He broke off acquaintance with all the gentry

353
00:22:51,944 --> 00:22:54,801
and shut himself up like a hermit at the hall.

354
00:22:55,860 --> 00:22:56,860
What?

355
00:22:57,440 --> 00:22:58,740
Did he not leave England?

356
00:23:00,100 --> 00:23:01,220
Leaving England?

357
00:23:01,699 --> 00:23:02,699
Bless you, no!

358
00:23:03,520 --> 00:23:09,580
He would not cross the door stones of the house, except at night when he walked just

359
00:23:09,581 --> 00:23:14,860
like a ghost about the grounds and in the orchard, as if he had lost his senses.

360
00:23:15,979 --> 00:23:20,740
Which it is my opinion he had, for a more spirited, bolder, keen

361
00:23:20,740 --> 00:23:24,279
gentleman than he was before that midge of a governess crossed him.

362
00:23:24,800 --> 00:23:26,279
You never saw, ma'am.

363
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:30,400
He was not a man given to wine or cards or

364
00:23:30,402 --> 00:23:33,880
racing a summer, and he was not so very handsome.

365
00:23:33,880 --> 00:23:39,100
But he had a courage and a will of his own, if ever man had.

366
00:23:39,820 --> 00:23:41,380
I knew him from a boy you see.

367
00:23:42,279 --> 00:23:45,921
And for my part, I have often wished that Miss Eyre had

368
00:23:45,922 --> 00:23:49,300
been sunk in the sea before she came to Thornfield Hall.

369
00:23:50,800 --> 00:23:54,160
Then Mr. Rochester was at home when the fire broke out.

370
00:23:55,419 --> 00:23:57,979
Yes, indeed was he.

371
00:23:58,940 --> 00:24:03,000
And he went up to the attics when all was burning above and below.

372
00:24:03,880 --> 00:24:07,611
And got the servants out of their beds and helped them down

373
00:24:07,611 --> 00:24:11,860
himself, and went back to get his mad wife out of her cell.

374
00:24:12,740 --> 00:24:18,160
And then they called out to him that she was on the roof, where she was standing,

375
00:24:18,479 --> 00:24:21,008
waving her arms above the battlements, and

376
00:24:21,009 --> 00:24:24,580
shouting out till they could hear her a mile off.

377
00:24:25,199 --> 00:24:28,600
I saw her and heard her with my own eyes.

378
00:24:29,440 --> 00:24:32,880
She was a big woman and had long black hair.

379
00:24:33,559 --> 00:24:36,520
We could see it streaming against the flames as she stood.

380
00:24:37,419 --> 00:24:41,149
I witnessed, and several more witnessed, Mr. Rochester

381
00:24:41,149 --> 00:24:44,040
ascend through the skylight onto the roof.

382
00:24:44,759 --> 00:24:46,419
We heard him call Bertha.

383
00:24:48,279 --> 00:24:54,300
We saw him approach her, and then, ma'am, she yelled and gave a spring and

384
00:24:54,301 --> 00:24:56,770
the next minute she lay smashed on the pavement.

385
00:24:57,770 --> 00:24:58,770
Dead?

386
00:24:59,050 --> 00:25:00,050
Dead?

387
00:25:00,229 --> 00:25:03,570
I dead as the stones on which her brains and blood were scattered.

388
00:25:04,229 --> 00:25:05,229
Good God!

389
00:25:05,910 --> 00:25:07,229
You may well say so, ma'am.

390
00:25:07,410 --> 00:25:08,410
It was frightful.

391
00:25:09,330 --> 00:25:10,330
He shuddered.

392
00:25:11,050 --> 00:25:13,169
And afterwards, I urged.

393
00:25:14,610 --> 00:25:17,270
Well, ma'am, afterwards, the house was burnt to the ground.

394
00:25:17,850 --> 00:25:19,690
There are only some bits of walls standing now.

395
00:25:20,889 --> 00:25:21,990
Were any other lives lost?

396
00:25:23,190 --> 00:25:24,190
No.

397
00:25:24,410 --> 00:25:26,350
Perhaps it would have been better if they had.

398
00:25:27,589 --> 00:25:28,589
What do you mean?

399
00:25:30,050 --> 00:25:31,449
Poor Mr. Edward.

400
00:25:32,210 --> 00:25:33,210
He ejaculated.

401
00:25:34,050 --> 00:25:36,050
I little thought ever to have seen it.

402
00:25:36,949 --> 00:25:41,970
Some say it was a just judgment on him for keeping his first marriage secret and

403
00:25:41,971 --> 00:25:44,429
wanting to take another wife while he had one living.

404
00:25:45,389 --> 00:25:47,070
But I pity him for my part.

405
00:25:48,389 --> 00:25:50,589
You said he was alive, I exclaimed.

406
00:25:51,130 --> 00:25:54,630
Yes, yes, he is alive, but many think he had better be dead.

407
00:25:55,449 --> 00:25:55,809
Why?

408
00:25:56,470 --> 00:25:57,470
How?

409
00:25:57,550 --> 00:25:58,869
My blood was again running cold.

410
00:25:59,550 --> 00:26:00,910
Where is he, I demanded.

411
00:26:01,490 --> 00:26:02,490
Is he in England?

412
00:26:03,130 --> 00:26:04,330
Aye, he's in England.

413
00:26:04,850 --> 00:26:06,330
He can't get out of England, I fancy.

414
00:26:06,669 --> 00:26:07,669
He's a fixture now.

415
00:26:09,089 --> 00:26:10,669
What agony was this?

416
00:26:11,610 --> 00:26:13,869
And the man seemed resolved to protract it.

417
00:26:14,910 --> 00:26:16,270
He is stone blind.

418
00:26:16,990 --> 00:26:17,990
He said it last.

419
00:26:18,410 --> 00:26:20,149
Yes, he is stone blind as Mr. Edward.

420
00:26:21,529 --> 00:26:23,410
I had dreaded worse.

421
00:26:24,889 --> 00:26:26,649
I had dreaded he was mad.

422
00:26:27,770 --> 00:26:31,029
I summoned strength to ask what had caused this calamity.

423
00:26:32,289 --> 00:26:37,610
It was all his own courage and a body may say his kindness in a way, man.

424
00:26:38,389 --> 00:26:41,770
He wouldn't leave the house till everyone else was out before him.

425
00:26:43,309 --> 00:26:48,809
As he came down the great staircase at last, after Mrs. Rochester had flung

426
00:26:48,811 --> 00:26:53,009
herself from the battlements, there was a great crash all fell.

427
00:26:54,210 --> 00:26:59,169
He was taken out from under the ruins, alive but sadly hurt.

428
00:27:00,429 --> 00:27:03,649
A beam had fallen in such a way as to protect him partly.

429
00:27:04,449 --> 00:27:05,949
But one eye was knocked out.

430
00:27:06,669 --> 00:27:12,990
And one hand so crushed so that Mr. Carter, the surgeon, had to amputate it directly.

431
00:27:13,149 --> 00:27:14,589
The other eye inflamed.

432
00:27:15,149 --> 00:27:16,570
He lost the sight of that also.

433
00:27:17,330 --> 00:27:18,850
He is now helpless indeed.

434
00:27:19,470 --> 00:27:20,470
Blind and a cripple.

435
00:27:21,550 --> 00:27:22,550
Where is he?

436
00:27:22,789 --> 00:27:23,789
Where does he now live?

437
00:27:24,830 --> 00:27:30,889
At Fondin, a man a house on a farm he has, about 30 miles off, quite a desolate spot.

438
00:27:31,729 --> 00:27:32,729
Who is with him?

439
00:27:33,789 --> 00:27:34,889
Old John and his wife.

440
00:27:35,669 --> 00:27:36,669
He would have none else.

441
00:27:37,070 --> 00:27:38,470
He's quite broken down, they say.

442
00:27:39,710 --> 00:27:41,289
Have you any sort of conveyance?

443
00:27:42,350 --> 00:27:45,289
We have a Shea's mom, a very handsome Shea's.

444
00:27:46,570 --> 00:27:48,509
Let it be got ready instantly.

445
00:27:49,429 --> 00:27:54,789
And if your post-boy can drive me to Fondin before dark this day, I'll pay both

446
00:27:54,790 --> 00:27:57,809
you and him twice the higher you usually demand.
